      "message": {
       "content": "# Research Agenda: Reducing Hallucinated Citations in Language-Model Outputs\n\n## Framing\n\n\"Hallucinated citation\" is not one failure mode. Before proposing fixes, the agenda must first decompose the phenomenon, then test interventions at the points where each sub-type originates. The five experiments below are sequenced so that each one's outcome gates or reshapes the next.\n\n---\n\n## Experiment 1 \u2014 Taxonomy and Baseline Characterisation\n\n**Hypothesis.**",
       "name": null,
       "reasoning": "The user wants a research agenda for reducing hallucinated citations in LLM outputs. They want 3-5 concrete experiments, each with:\n- Hypothesis\n- Datasets (or how to build them)\n- Metrics\n- Expected outcomes\n- What each result would change about the next step\n\nLet me think carefully about what \"hallucinated citations\" means and what a rigorous research agenda would look like.\n\nHallucinated citations = the model outputs a reference (author, title, journal, year, DOI, page numbers) that either:\n1. Doesn't exist at all (fabricated)\n2. Exists but the model attributes the wrong content to it (misattribution)\n3. Exists but the model gets metadata wrong (wrong year, wrong journal, wrong authors)\n4. The model cites a real paper but the specific claim it attributes to that paper isn't actually in it\n\nLet me design 3-5 experiments that build on each other logically:\n\nExperiment 1: Baseline characterization / taxonomy of hallucination types\n- Need to understand the landscape before fixing it\n- Build a dataset of prompts that elicit citations, then classify the types of hallucination\n- This tells us WHERE the problem is\n\nExperiment 2: Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) with verification\n- Test whether grounding in a real corpus reduces hallucination\n- But also test whether the model still hallucinates even when given relevant documents\n- This tests the \"grounding\" hypothesis\n\nExperiment 3: Self-verification / self-correction\n- Test whether the model can detect its own hallucinated citations\n- Or whether a second pass with a verifier model helps\n- This tests the \"can we catch it after the fact\" hypothesis\n\nExperiment 4: Training-time intervention (RLHF or fine-tuning with citation-accuracy reward)\n- Train a model with a reward signal that penalizes unverifiable citations\n- Test whether this reduces hallucination without degrading",
       "reasoning_content": "The user wants a research agenda for reducing hallucinated citations in LLM outputs. They want 3-5 concrete experiments, each with:\n- Hypothesis\n- Datasets (or how to build them)\n- Metrics\n- Expected outcomes\n- What each result would change about the next step\n\nLet me think carefully about what \"hallucinated citations\" means and what a rigorous research agenda would look like.\n\nHallucinated citations = the model outputs a reference (author, title, journal, year, DOI, page numbers) that either:\n1. Doesn't exist at all (fabricated)\n2. Exists but the model attributes the wrong content to it (misattribution)\n3. Exists but the model gets metadata wrong (wrong year, wrong journal, wrong authors)\n4. The model cites a real paper but the specific claim it attributes to that paper isn't actually in it\n\nLet me design 3-5 experiments that build on each other logically:\n\nExperiment 1: Baseline characterization / taxonomy of hallucination types\n- Need to understand the landscape before fixing it\n- Build a dataset of prompts that elicit citations, then classify the types of hallucination\n- This tells us WHERE the problem is\n\nExperiment 2: Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) with verification\n- Test whether grounding in a real corpus reduces hallucination\n- But also test whether the model still hallucinates even when given relevant documents\n- This tests the \"grounding\" hypothesis\n\nExperiment 3: Self-verification / self-correction\n- Test whether the model can detect its own hallucinated citations\n- Or whether a second pass with a verifier model helps\n- This tests the \"can we catch it after the fact\" hypothesis\n\nExperiment 4: Training-time intervention (RLHF or fine-tuning with citation-accuracy reward)\n- Train a model with a reward signal that penalizes unverifiable citations\n- Test whether this reduces hallucination without degrading",
